# Manifest customization

The Default Manifest
--------------------

The default manifest is built by `ManifestBuilder` based on the `<archive>` configuration.
By default, it includes the `Java-Version` attribute (when the compiler target is discoverable from the POM).
`Created-By` is always present in the final JAR — set by `ToolExecutor` to a JDK-independent string for reproducible builds, or supplied by the `jar` tool itself otherwise.
When `addBuildEnvironmentEntries` is enabled, `Built-By`, `Build-Jdk`, and `Build-Jdk-Spec` are also added.
To add Specification and Implementation details or a classpath, configure them explicitly in the `<archive>` element.

The `<archive>` Configuration Element
--------------------------------------

The `<archive>` element accepts the following top-level options:

| Field | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `compress` | boolean | `true` | Whether to compress the JAR file entries. |
| `addMavenDescriptor` | boolean | `true` | Whether to include the Maven POM descriptor (`META-INF/maven/…/pom.xml` and `pom.properties`) in the JAR. |
| `forced` | boolean | `true` | Whether to recreate the archive even when up to date. |
| `manifestFile` | Path | — | Path to an existing manifest file to use as a base. Its entries are merged with any generated entries; explicitly configured values take precedence. |
| `manifest` | ManifestConfig | — | Manifest-specific configuration; see below. |
| `manifestEntries` | Map | — | Arbitrary key/value pairs to add to the main manifest section. |
| `manifestSections` | List | — | Additional named manifest sections; each must have a `<name>` and `<manifestEntries>`. |
| `pomPropertiesFile` | Path | — | Path to an existing `pom.properties` file to embed in the JAR. When set, overrides the auto-generated one. |

### `<manifest>` Options

| Field | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `mainClass` | String | — | The `Main-Class` attribute value. Sets the entry point for executable JARs. |
| `addClasspath` | boolean | `false` | Whether to generate a `Class-Path` attribute from the project's runtime dependencies. |
| `classpathPrefix` | String | `""` | A prefix prepended to every entry in the generated `Class-Path`. |
| `classpathLayoutType` | String | `"simple"` | Layout type for `Class-Path` entries. One of `simple`, `repository`, or `custom`. |
| `customClasspathLayout` | String | — | Custom layout template when `classpathLayoutType` is `custom`. Supports `\${artifact.*}` expressions (see below). |
| `useUniqueVersions` | boolean | `true` | Whether to use unique timestamps for SNAPSHOT artifacts in `Class-Path` entries. |
| `addDefaultEntries` | boolean | `true` | Whether to add the `Java-Version` attribute (sourced from the compiler plugin's `release`/`target` setting). |
| `addBuildEnvironmentEntries` | boolean | `false` | Whether to add `Build-Jdk-Spec`, `Build-Jdk`, and `Built-By` attributes from the current JVM. |
| `addDefaultSpecificationEntries` | boolean | `false` | Whether to add `Specification-Title`, `Specification-Version`, and `Specification-Vendor` from the POM. |
| `addDefaultImplementationEntries` | boolean | `false` | Whether to add `Implementation-Title`, `Implementation-Version`, `Implementation-Vendor`, `Implementation-Vendor-Id`, and `Implementation-URL` from the POM. |

### Classpath Layout Expressions

When `classpathLayoutType` is `custom`, the `customClasspathLayout` value is a template that may reference
the following `\${artifact.*}` expressions for each runtime dependency:

| Expression | Description |
|---|---|
| `\${artifact.groupId}` | The dependency's group ID. |
| `\${artifact.groupIdPath}` | The group ID with `.` replaced by `/` (for repository-style paths). |
| `\${artifact.artifactId}` | The dependency's artifact ID. |
| `\${artifact.version}` | The dependency's version (unique SNAPSHOT timestamp when applicable). |
| `\${artifact.baseVersion}` | The dependency's base version (e.g. `1.0-SNAPSHOT`). |
| `\${artifact.extension}` | The dependency's file extension (e.g. `jar`). |
| `\${artifact.classifier}` | The dependency's classifier, or empty string if absent. |
| `\${dashClassifier?}` | `-\${classifier}` if a classifier is present, otherwise empty. |

The `repository` layout type is equivalent to:
`\${artifact.groupIdPath}/\${artifact.artifactId}/\${artifact.baseVersion}/\${artifact.artifactId}-\${artifact.version}\${dashClassifier?}.\${artifact.extension}`

The `simple` layout type is equivalent to:
`\${artifact.artifactId}-\${artifact.version}\${dashClassifier?}.\${artifact.extension}`

Manifest Customization
----------------------

You can alter the default manifest with the `archive` configuration element.
The example below shows some of the available configuration options.
For more information, see the [plugin documentation](../plugin-info.html).

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <index>true</index>
            <manifest>
              <addClasspath>true</addClasspath>
            </manifest>
            <manifestEntries>
              <mode>development</mode>
              <url>${esc.d}{project.url}</url>
              <key>value</key>
            </manifestEntries>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
        ...
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

Making an Executable JAR
------------------------

To create an executable JAR, set the `Main-Class` attribute:

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <configuration>
    <archive>
      <manifest>
        <mainClass>com.example.Main</mainClass>
      </manifest>
    </archive>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```

Adding a Class-Path Attribute
------------------------------

To add a `Class-Path` attribute listing the project's runtime dependencies (useful for thin JARs
that load dependencies from the file system at runtime):

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <configuration>
    <archive>
      <manifest>
        <addClasspath>true</addClasspath>
        <classpathPrefix>lib/</classpathPrefix>
      </manifest>
    </archive>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```

This generates a manifest entry similar to:
```
Class-Path: lib/commons-lang3-3.12.0.jar lib/guava-31.0-jre.jar
```

Adding Specification and Implementation Entries
------------------------------------------------

To include version and vendor metadata from the POM:

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <configuration>
    <archive>
      <manifest>
        <addDefaultSpecificationEntries>true</addDefaultSpecificationEntries>
        <addDefaultImplementationEntries>true</addDefaultImplementationEntries>
      </manifest>
    </archive>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```

This adds attributes sourced from the POM's `<name>`, `<version>`, `<organization>`, `<groupId>`, and `<url>` elements.

Adding Additional Manifest Sections
-------------------------------------

Named manifest sections (used by Java extensions and some frameworks) can be added via `<manifestSections>`:

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <configuration>
    <archive>
      <manifestSections>
        <manifestSection>
          <name>com/example/MyClass.class</name>
          <manifestEntries>
            <Sealed>true</Sealed>
          </manifestEntries>
        </manifestSection>
      </manifestSections>
    </archive>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```

Using an Existing Manifest File
---------------------------------

To base the manifest on an existing file and augment it with computed entries:

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <configuration>
    <archive>
      <manifestFile>src/main/resources/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF</manifestFile>
    </archive>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```
